// BUG-ZYB-010 — a `:>` runs when the `!?` block returns.
//
// "Always runs" has to include the case where the try block returns, which is
// the case a cleanup clause exists for. It did not:
//
//   - the register VM emitted the finally inline after the try/catch, so a
//     `<~` returned from the function and jumped clean over those
//     instructions — the finally never ran at all;
//   - the tree-walker ran it with the pending return still set, and
//     `execute_block` stops at the first statement while control flow is
//     pending, so `>> "limpieza" ¶` printed the text and swallowed the
//     newline. Half a statement, which is worse than none, because it looks
//     like it worked.
//
// The browser engine was right all along, which is what made it a divergence
// rather than a missing feature.
//
// A `<~` written INSIDE the `:>` is covered too, at the foot of this file:
// it is discarded (BUG-ZYB-011).

// Two levels: the inner cleanup first, then the outer.
anidado() {
    !? {
        !? {
            <~ "vuelvo"
        } :> {
            >> "interno "
        }
    } :> {
        >> "externo "
    }
}
>> anidado() ¶

// The value is computed before the finally runs, so a finally touching the
// same name cannot change what comes back.
valor_fijado() {
    v = "original"
    !? {
        <~ v
    } :> {
        v = "cambiado"
    }
}
>> valor_fijado() ¶

// Tail recursion inside a try with a finally: the tail call is suppressed —
// a frame that still owes cleanup must not be replaced — so the finally runs
// once per turn.
cuenta(n) {
    !? {
        ? n <= 0 { <~ "fin" }
        <~ cuenta(n - 1)
    } :> {
        >> "."
    }
}
>> cuenta(3) ¶

// A return from inside a catch clause owes the finally too.
con_captura() {
    !? {
        <~ [1][99]
    } :! {
        <~ "capturado"
    } :> {
        >> "limpio "
    }
}
>> con_captura() ¶

// And tail recursion with no try at all keeps its tail call: 100000 frames
// deep would not fit on the stack otherwise, so this line is the check that
// the suppression above stayed inside the try.
suma(n, acc) {
    ? n <= 0 { <~ acc }
    <~ suma(n - 1, acc + n)
}
>> suma(100000, 0) ¶

// BUG-ZYB-011 — a `:>` is cleanup, and cleanup does not decide what comes back.
//
// A `<~` inside the finally evaluates its expression and is then discarded; the
// return the try block was carrying continues. Java and Python let the finally
// win and both warn against relying on it in their own style guides — Zymbol
// takes the warning instead of the feature, so a reader can trust that the
// value coming back is the one at the `<~` they can see.
//
// The three engines gave two answers before this: the Rust pair returned from
// the finally, the browser engine carried on.
solo_limpieza() {
    !? {
        _x = 1
    } :> {
        <~ "desde el finally"
    }
    <~ "después del try"
}
>> solo_limpieza() ¶

// The return the try block carries survives the finally untouched.
con_retorno() {
    !? {
        <~ "del try"
    } :> {
        <~ "del finally"
    }
}
>> con_retorno() ¶

// And the expression still runs, for whatever it does on the way.
marca(x) {
    >> "  evaluado: " x ¶
    <~ x
}
efecto() {
    !? {
        <~ "del try"
    } :> {
        <~ marca("!")
    }
}
>> efecto() ¶
